00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:03.659
In this video, I'm going to demonstrate
some of the structural formatting tools

00:00:03.659 --> 00:00:06.810
that you can use to enhance the meaning
and appearance of your presentation.

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:12.509
As you can see, the presentation that I
have on screen at the moment contains four

00:00:12.509 --> 00:00:17.820
slides and there is no real definition
to the layout and the different sections

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:22.350
within each of the slides other than
having two images on the title slide.

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:26.670
So when you're creating a presentation,
the first thing you need to do is to

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:30.630
make sure that you select the right
layout for the slide that you're

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:32.220
going to include the content in.

00:00:32.730 --> 00:00:35.589
So for example, on slide
one, this is a title slide.

00:00:35.610 --> 00:00:40.230
So if I just click on that and go
to the home tab and then select

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:43.359
a slide layout button, I can
see that that is a title slide.

00:00:43.739 --> 00:00:47.350
For the second one, I have
used a two content slide.

00:00:48.270 --> 00:00:50.970
For the third slide, it
is a title and content.

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:54.990
And for the fourth slide again,
it is a title and content.

00:00:56.100 --> 00:01:00.480
So it is important that you try and
select the correct layout for the

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:02.010
information that you need to include.

00:01:02.490 --> 00:01:07.020
However, it may be a case at a later
point in the design and development

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:10.840
of your presentation that you
wish to change the slide layout.

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:17.265
So for example, on slide three, at
the moment, I have some information

00:01:17.265 --> 00:01:20.655
in here that may be enhanced
by the inclusion of a chart.

00:01:21.735 --> 00:01:25.335
So at this point, what I'd need to do is
to change the actual layout of the slide.

00:01:25.785 --> 00:01:28.765
So now that  I've got the slide
highlighted, if I go up to the

00:01:28.994 --> 00:01:34.230
layout and then selected two content
that will automatically change.

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:37.620
So as you can see, it has compressed
the information that was already on

00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:41.490
the slide to the left, which that will
then allow me to add the different

00:01:41.490 --> 00:01:42.990
type of information to the right.

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:46.650
So I could then select chart or
a table or some other type of

00:01:46.650 --> 00:01:49.270
information and add that to the slide.

00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:52.310
So I'll just undo that.

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:58.729
There also may be occasions where you
may need to change the slide order.

00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:02.570
So when you're doing a presentation,
you may think that slide three will

00:02:02.570 --> 00:02:06.740
be better as slide four, slide five
or slide six and so on and so forth.

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:09.410
It's quite straightforward
within PowerPoint to change

00:02:09.410 --> 00:02:10.140
the order of the slides.

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:13.410
All you would need to do is as
an example, I've selected slide

00:02:13.490 --> 00:02:14.930
two, and I want to change that.

00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:18.120
So it'll change places with
slide three, just click and

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:20.100
hold and then drag that down.

00:02:20.550 --> 00:02:25.140
And again, if I wanted to change slide
four to slide three, just click and hold

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:27.180
and drag it to where I wish it to be.

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:33.900
I can then change them back and I have
the original order presented again.

00:02:36.405 --> 00:02:39.555
As you can see the slides at the
moment are quite plain and have

00:02:39.555 --> 00:02:42.525
a white background, there's text
there, but there is no definition.

00:02:42.615 --> 00:02:44.995
The information hasn't
been broken up in any way.

00:02:45.915 --> 00:02:49.245
One of the other tools that you can
use is you can break each section down.

00:02:49.245 --> 00:02:53.475
So for example, I'm going to use the
business growth slide to demonstrate this.

00:02:53.985 --> 00:02:55.455
So I have the title section here.

00:02:55.455 --> 00:02:58.690
So I click on the title,
the box appears around it.

00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:01.750
So I can then add a border to that.

00:03:02.050 --> 00:03:04.510
And I'm just going to
select a plain black border.

00:03:05.110 --> 00:03:12.520
And again, for the information to the
left of the chart, I will select a border.

00:03:12.820 --> 00:03:19.028
And for the chart, I'll do exactly the
same, select a border around the chart.

00:03:19.433 --> 00:03:22.163
So, as you can see, that has
now started to define the

00:03:22.163 --> 00:03:24.173
information within this slide.

00:03:24.953 --> 00:03:28.523
I can also take that one step
further and if highlight that

00:03:28.523 --> 00:03:29.783
I can actually add a fill.

00:03:30.293 --> 00:03:32.783
So I can select a color
to fill the background.

00:03:32.903 --> 00:03:34.823
That's quite dramatic.

00:03:34.853 --> 00:03:40.743
I think I'll go for that shade there,
again, I could do this same shade so, the

00:03:40.743 --> 00:03:44.753
last shade selected will automatically
be the one by default that you can

00:03:44.753 --> 00:03:50.333
select, but again, drop down, but I'll
stick with the shade that I went with.

00:03:50.793 --> 00:03:53.633
But for the chart, all I'm
going to do is define that by,

00:03:54.093 --> 00:03:55.613
I may make the border thicker.

00:03:55.643 --> 00:04:00.883
So again, the border was quite thin
so I can select weight and then I can

00:04:00.883 --> 00:04:03.943
put a thicker border around the chart.

00:04:04.243 --> 00:04:07.243
So as you can see, that has
slowly broken that information up.

00:04:07.603 --> 00:04:10.483
So each section has got
its own defined area.

00:04:12.878 --> 00:04:16.028
When creating presentations,
there may also be occasions where

00:04:16.028 --> 00:04:18.068
you need to hide certain slides.

00:04:18.188 --> 00:04:23.318
So for example, it could be a presentation
that has been presented to members

00:04:23.318 --> 00:04:28.178
of the staff and also managers within
an organization, but rather than have

00:04:28.178 --> 00:04:32.198
two different presentations, you've
created one that has additional

00:04:32.198 --> 00:04:34.028
information in for the managers.

00:04:34.508 --> 00:04:37.238
And when you present that to
the staff, you need to make sure

00:04:37.238 --> 00:04:38.608
that they can't see those slides.

00:04:38.888 --> 00:04:41.708
So quite simply you can hide the
slides within the presentation.

00:04:42.308 --> 00:04:45.448
And to do that, all you would do is
highlight the slide that you wish to hide.

00:04:45.998 --> 00:04:48.638
Right click and then select hide slide.

00:04:49.118 --> 00:04:53.588
So when that was presented slide two, as
it has a strike through there, you would

00:04:53.588 --> 00:04:55.448
see that that would not be displayed.

00:04:55.448 --> 00:04:58.868
So with, in the presentation, it
goes slide one and then jump straight

00:04:58.868 --> 00:05:00.398
to slide three and slide four.

00:05:01.118 --> 00:05:04.988
And then when you decide that you need to
present that with all of the slideshow,

00:05:05.018 --> 00:05:05.868
you would simply right click again.

00:05:05.868 --> 00:05:10.861
Select hide slide, and that will take
the strike-through away from the number.

00:05:11.221 --> 00:05:13.921
So in the presentation, it
would go one, two, three, and

00:05:13.921 --> 00:05:15.531
four with no slides omitted.

00:05:17.851 --> 00:05:20.731
As with Word, you also have the
opportunity to use headers and

00:05:20.731 --> 00:05:24.561
footers and within PowerPoint to
access the headers and footers, you

00:05:24.561 --> 00:05:29.071
would simply go to the insert tab
and then select headers and footers.

00:05:29.611 --> 00:05:33.826
And it may be a case that you wish to
put the date and time in and have that

00:05:33.856 --> 00:05:37.456
updated automatically, or it could be
fixed so if I select date and time.

00:05:37.876 --> 00:05:40.936
So whenever the presentation is
displayed, it would automatically

00:05:40.936 --> 00:05:45.976
change the date to that date that it was
presented, or you could have it fixed.

00:05:45.976 --> 00:05:49.636
So if you wanted to have it fixed so it
displayed the date, when the presentation

00:05:49.636 --> 00:05:54.436
was created, you would select that
option, you can also select slide number.

00:05:54.826 --> 00:05:57.166
And if you look along the right and
the preview pane, it's showing you

00:05:57.196 --> 00:05:59.686
exactly where that information would be.

00:06:00.196 --> 00:06:04.066
Additionally, you may also
wish to have the footer active.

00:06:04.096 --> 00:06:08.116
So that could be a case of you maybe
need to put the, the company name in

00:06:08.116 --> 00:06:10.396
there or the author of the presentation.

00:06:10.756 --> 00:06:15.526
So if I select that, now I have the option
to either select it so it doesn't show

00:06:15.526 --> 00:06:20.296
on the title slide but I want it to show
on every slide and I can apply that, but

00:06:20.296 --> 00:06:22.276
it will only apply it to that one slide.

00:06:22.966 --> 00:06:26.686
Alternatively, I can select apply
to all, and that will apply that

00:06:26.686 --> 00:06:28.276
to every slide in the presentation.

00:06:28.276 --> 00:06:31.996
So if I select that now, as you
can see, it has applied that

00:06:32.506 --> 00:06:34.876
to each of the four slides.

00:06:37.981 --> 00:06:42.001
Primarily most slides within a
presentation are in landscape format.

00:06:42.001 --> 00:06:44.881
And most presentations are
presented in landscape format.

00:06:45.361 --> 00:06:50.131
However, there may be occasions where
you need to change it to portrait format.

00:06:50.791 --> 00:06:54.031
Now, within portrait format, one of
the things you have to be aware of

00:06:54.031 --> 00:06:57.421
is that when you change one slide to
portrait, unlike Word, where you could

00:06:57.421 --> 00:07:02.161
have one page in landscape, one page in
portrait and other page in landscape,

00:07:02.521 --> 00:07:06.691
within PowerPoint, if you change the
orientation of one slide, it will change

00:07:06.691 --> 00:07:08.491
the orientation of all the slides.

00:07:08.911 --> 00:07:13.501
So just to demonstrate that if I
have the title slide selected, and

00:07:13.501 --> 00:07:15.031
then I need to go to the design tab.

00:07:15.501 --> 00:07:19.876
And on the design tab select slide
size and at the moment you've got

00:07:19.876 --> 00:07:23.896
standard or widescreen, but you would
need to select custom slide size.

00:07:24.436 --> 00:07:27.466
And from there you would
need to select portrait.

00:07:27.976 --> 00:07:31.726
So if I click portrait and
hit OK, you get two options.

00:07:31.756 --> 00:07:34.876
So you can either maximize
the information or ensure fit.

00:07:34.906 --> 00:07:39.286
If you select maximize, it would basically
change the page orientation, but the

00:07:39.286 --> 00:07:44.266
information within won't change, So if
I do that now, select, maximize, and

00:07:44.266 --> 00:07:49.141
then select that, as you can see, that
isn't really fit for purpose because

00:07:49.141 --> 00:07:54.591
it's changed the slides to portrait, but
the information is still in landscape.

00:07:54.591 --> 00:08:01.101
So if I just undo that and again,
go back to the slide size, custom

00:08:01.101 --> 00:08:06.781
slide fit, select portrait, select
OK, but this time select ensure fit.

00:08:07.861 --> 00:08:12.931
As you can see that has then ensured that
the information does fit on the slide.

